1:# Divider drag: kill flicker + make the deck move at 60fps
2:
3:## Context
4:Dragging the pane divider still looks bad in two ways the PTY-resize-claim fix did not touch: (1) the divider line **flickers and changes color** during the drag, and (2) the whole deck (terminals + strips) **doesn't move smoothly** — sub-60fps jank, worse on faster drags. Both stem from per-drag-frame DOM churn in `TerminalDeck.tsx`: the dividers `<For>` recreates the divider node every frame, and unthrottled `pointermove` runs the full layout memo each event while `samePaneView` (rect-sensitive) forces the strips `<For>` to re-mount every `PaneStrip` per frame. End state: the divider node is stable and holds a steady highlight while dragged; ratio updates are coalesced to one per animation frame; strip nodes are not re-mounted by a rect-only change.
5:
6:## Approach
7:Two behavior groups. Group A and Group B are independent; the tree builds after either. Within Group A the component prop change and its callsite MUST land together.
8:
9:### Group A — Divider: stable node, steady color, rAF-coalesced updates
10:Fixes the flicker (recreation-driven) and the divider's contribution to jank. Reuses the existing `beginResizeDrag`/`endResizeDrag` (`apps/web/src/lib/resizeDrag.ts`) already wired in. No existing rAF-coalescing helper for pointer drags exists — inline it (the `windowSizeClass.ts` rAF-debounce is for window resize, not a drag gesture, so it is not reusable here).
11:
12:1. **Switch the dividers list from `<For>` to `<Index>`** in `TerminalDeck.tsx`. `<Index>` keeps one DOM node per list slot and updates its content reactively, so the node is NOT recreated each frame (the divider count is fixed during a drag). Add `Index` to the `solid-js` import on line 10 (currently `import { For, Show, createMemo, createSignal, createEffect, onMount, onCleanup } from "solid-js";`). Change the render block (currently lines 355–357):
13:   ```tsx
14:   <Index each={view().dividers}>
15:     {(d) => <PaneDivider divider={d} deckEl={() => deckEl} onDrag={onDividerDrag} onCommit={onDividerCommit} />}
16:   </Index>
17:   ```
18:   `d` is `Accessor<DividerRect>`. The `panes` strips `<For>` above it stays a `<For>` (Group B handles its churn differently — see rationale there).
19:
20:2. **Rewrite `PaneDivider.tsx`** to take an accessor prop, coalesce moves via rAF, and expose a per-divider dragging flag. Full replacement of the component (it is small; every `props.divider.X` becomes `props.divider().X`):
21:   ```tsx
22:   import type { Accessor } from "solid-js";
23:   import { createSignal } from "solid-js";
24:   import type { DividerRect } from "../store/paneLayout.ts";
25:   import { beginResizeDrag, endResizeDrag } from "../lib/resizeDrag.ts";
26:
27:   // Draggable split divider on the seam between two panes. Under <Index> the
28:   // node is STABLE across drag frames (only its position updates reactively),
29:   // so a per-divider `dragging` signal drives a steady highlight and pointer
30:   // state survives. Ratio updates are coalesced to one per animation frame so
31:   // a 120Hz+ trackpad can't run the deck layout more than once per paint.
32:   // Listens on WINDOW (not the element) so the gesture is robust even if the
33:   // node is ever replaced. Callers: TerminalDeck.tsx (one per split).
34:   export function PaneDivider(props: {
35:     divider: Accessor<DividerRect>;
36:     deckEl: () => HTMLElement | undefined;
37:     onDrag: (splitId: string, ratio: number) => void;
38:     onCommit: (splitId: string, ratio: number) => void;
39:   }) {
40:     const [dragging, setDragging] = createSignal(false);
41:     function onPointerDown(e: PointerEvent) {
42:       if (e.button !== 0) return;
43:       e.preventDefault();
44:       e.stopPropagation(); // don't let the deck treat this as a focus-pane click
45:       beginResizeDrag();
46:       setDragging(true);
47:       // Snapshot: regionStart/regionLen/dir/splitId are fixed for the whole drag.
48:       const d = props.divider();
49:       const onDrag = props.onDrag;
50:       const onCommit = props.onCommit;
51:       const rect = props.deckEl()?.getBoundingClientRect();
52:       const originX = rect?.left ?? 0;
53:       const originY = rect?.top ?? 0;
54:       let last = d.ratio;
55:       let rafId = 0;
56:       const ratioFor = (ev: PointerEvent): number => {
57:         const pos = d.dir === "row" ? ev.clientX - originX : ev.clientY - originY;
58:         const raw = d.regionLen > 0 ? (pos - d.regionStart) / d.regionLen : 0.5;
59:         return Math.max(0.1, Math.min(0.9, raw));
60:       };
61:       // Coalesce pointermove → one layout update per frame; latest ratio wins.
62:       const flush = () => { rafId = 0; onDrag(d.splitId, last); };
63:       const onMove = (ev: PointerEvent) => {
64:         last = ratioFor(ev);
65:         if (!rafId) rafId = requestAnimationFrame(flush);
66:       };
67:       const onUp = () => {
68:         if (rafId) { cancelAnimationFrame(rafId); rafId = 0; }
69:         setDragging(false);
70:         endResizeDrag();
71:         onCommit(d.splitId, last);
72:         window.removeEventListener("pointermove", onMove);
73:         window.removeEventListener("pointerup", onUp);
74:         window.removeEventListener("pointercancel", onUp);
75:       };
76:       window.addEventListener("pointermove", onMove);
77:       window.addEventListener("pointerup", onUp);
78:       window.addEventListener("pointercancel", onUp);
79:     }
80:
81:     return (
82:       <div
83:         data-testid={`pane-divider-${props.divider().splitId}`}
84:         class="pane-divider"
85:         data-dir={props.divider().dir}
86:         data-dragging={dragging() ? "true" : undefined}
87:         onPointerDown={onPointerDown}
88:         style={{
89:           position: "absolute",
90:           left: `${props.divider().x}px`,
91:           top: `${props.divider().y}px`,
92:           width: `${props.divider().w}px`,
93:           height: `${props.divider().h}px`,
94:           cursor: props.divider().dir === "row" ? "col-resize" : "row-resize",
95:           "z-index": "4",
96:           "touch-action": "none",
97:         }}
98:       />
99:     );
100:   }
101:   ```
102:   Note: `cancelAnimationFrame` on `onUp` is REQUIRED — a stray `flush` after `onCommit` cleared the transient `dragRatios` would re-insert a ratio and leave the split visually stuck.
103:
104:3. **Add a deterministic drag-highlight CSS rule** in `apps/web/src/styles/sidebar.css` immediately after the existing `.pane-divider:hover, .pane-divider:active { … }` block (ends at line 1278). Keep the existing hover/active rules (non-drag affordance):
105:   ```css
106:   .pane-divider[data-dragging="true"] {
107:     opacity: 1;
108:     background: var(--md-sys-color-primary);
109:     transition: none;
110:   }
111:   ```
112:   `transition: none` + the attribute being set the instant the drag starts makes the color snap to primary and hold steady (no 120ms re-animation, no dependence on `:hover`/`:active` which drop when the pointer leaves the 6px strip).
113:
114:### Group B — Strips: don't re-mount on a rect-only change
115:Fixes the strips' contribution to jank. `PaneStrip` is stateful (M3 sliding-indicator effect, drag-reorder state), so rather than rewire its many props to `<Index>` accessors (risk), keep the `<For>` and stop rect-only changes from producing a new `PaneView` ref. Position comes from a separate live rect map.
116:
117:1. **Drop rect from `samePaneView`** (`TerminalDeck.tsx` lines 40–44) so a drag (rect-only change) reuses the cached `PaneView` ref → the strips `<For>` does not re-mount:
118:   ```ts
119:   function samePaneView(a: PaneView, b: PaneView): boolean {
120:     return a.paneId === b.paneId && a.selectedTab === b.selectedTab && a.focused === b.focused
121:       && a.tabIds.length === b.tabIds.length && a.tabIds.every((id, i) => id === b.tabIds[i]);
122:   }
123:   ```
124:   Update its comment: identity no longer tracks rect; position is read

